!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/meta.tn?sha256=ee2394b1554c4ea15cee5441b1622f8ddee19c171a7425b07aeab63da9fafc0a"
@doc:"""
  The TSON kernel: the vocabulary every schema is written in. Its root holds the
  names an author writes: `text`, `integer`, `int64`, `boolean`, `bytes`, `iri`,
  `iri_reference`, `regex`, `void`, the `enum` constructor, `set<T>`, `@doc` and
  the `schema` tag. Its maps hold the machinery behind them: the type system
  (`meta`), the atom constructors (`numeric`, `unicode`, `net`, `binary`) and
  the classifying annotations (`atom`).

  Every processor pre-loads it ([TSON-SCHEMA] §3.4). §9 lists what each bundled
  document holds, and §5 is the grammar the examples here are written in.
  """
!schema {

  numeric => !schema {
    @doc:"""
      A fixed width for an integer: `size: { bits: 32  signed: true }`. Signed
      n-bit is [-2^(n-1), 2^(n-1)-1], unsigned [0, 2^n-1]. `bits` is above zero.
      """
    integer_size => {
      bits:    integer
      signed:  boolean
    }

    @doc:"The members of an integer_type."
    integer_member_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: integer  min_items: 1 }

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of `integer`. An author refines the instance,
      `age => !integer ^ { min: 0  max: 150 }`, with bounds, a width (`size`), a
      step (`multiple_of`) or the admitted values (`members`).

      A bound lies inside the range `size` gives, and the lower bound does not
      exceed the upper. `multiple_of` is strictly positive and tests the value's
      magnitude, so -30 is a multiple of 15. Every member satisfies the other
      facets on the same body ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5; §5.7 for what a refinement
      may tighten).
      """
    integer_type => meta.atom & {
      size?: numeric.integer_size
      ( min: integer | exclusive_min: integer )?
      ( max: integer | exclusive_max: integer )?
      multiple_of?: integer
      members?:     numeric.integer_member_set
    }

    @doc:"""
      A non-negative `int64`: the type of every facet that counts something, a
      length, an item count, a digit count.
      """
    count => !int64 ^ { min: 0 }
  }

  unicode => !schema {
    @doc:"The members of a text_type."
    text_member_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: text  min_items: 1 }

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of `text`. An author refines the instance:
      `name => !text ^ { min_length: 1  max_length: 80 }`,
      `code => !text ^ { pattern: "[A-Z]{3}" }`. Lengths count code points,
      `pattern` is an I-Regexp (RFC 9485) the whole value matches, and `members`
      lists the admitted strings.

      A value is its token's text put into `normalization`'s form, and every
      other facet, and every comparison of two values, judges that value and
      never the spelling ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5; §5.7 for what a refinement may
      tighten).
      """
    text_type => meta.atom & {
      min_length?:    numeric.count
      max_length?:    numeric.count
      length?:        numeric.count
      pattern?:       regex
      members?:       unicode.text_member_set
      normalization?: unicode.normalization ~ NONE
    }

    @doc:"""
      What a profile's Start or Continue set is drawn from (UAX #31): XID_Start
      and XID_Continue, ID_Start and ID_Continue, their ASCII members (Start A-Z
      a-z, Continue adding 0-9 and "_"), or the empty set.
      """
    identifier_base => !enum [XID ID ASCII NONE]

    @doc:"""
      The form a text value is put into. NONE keeps the text as written.
      NFKC_CASEFOLD is toNFKC_Casefold. ASCII_CASEFOLD maps A-Z to a-z and
      changes nothing else ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5).
      """
    normalization => !enum [NONE NFC NFKC NFKC_CASEFOLD ASCII_CASEFOLD]

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of an identifier family: a UAX #31 profile (R1), with
      text_type's facets applied inside it. `meta.identifier` is the kernel's,
      and a schema declares its own for another naming convention,
      `snake => !unicode.identifier_type { start: ASCII  continue: ASCII }`:

        identifier := Start Continue* (Medial Continue+)*
        Start      := (start ∪ start_add) − exclude
        Continue   := (continue ∪ continue_add) − exclude
        Medial     := medial

      `start_add`, `continue_add`, `medial` and `exclude` are each the set of
      code points their text holds, and `medial` is disjoint from Start and
      Continue. The profile judges the value, the text in its normalization
      form. A join control (U+200C, U+200D) is admitted only in the contexts UTS
      #39 §3.1.1.1 permits, whatever the profile ([TSON-DATA] §7.7,
      [TSON-SCHEMA] §7.4).
      """
    identifier_type => unicode.text_type & atom.specification & {
      spec?:          = "https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr31/"
      start?:         unicode.identifier_base ~ XID
      continue?:      unicode.identifier_base ~ XID
      start_add?:     text
      continue_add?:  text
      medial?:        text
      exclude?:       text
      normalization?: unicode.normalization ~ NFC
    }

    @doc:"The constructor of `regex`: an I-Regexp (RFC 9485), kept as written."
    regex_type => unicode.text_type & atom.specification & {
      spec?:          = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9485"
      normalization?: = NONE
    }
  }

  net => !schema {
    @doc:"The schemes an iri_type or uri_type admits."
    scheme_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: net.scheme_name  min_items: 1 }

    @doc:"A URI scheme (RFC 3986 §3.1), compared without case."
    scheme_name => !unicode.identifier_type { start: ASCII  continue: ASCII
      continue_add: "+-."  exclude: "_"  normalization: ASCII_CASEFOLD }

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of `iri` and `iri_reference` (RFC 3987). `schemes` lists
      the admitted schemes, `web => !iri ^ { schemes: [https] }`, and a value
      with no scheme is outside every list. `allow_relative` admits a relative
      reference and `allow_fragment` a fragment. An IRI is judged through the
      URI it maps to (RFC 3987 §3.1).
      """
    iri_type => unicode.text_type & atom.specification & {
      spec?:           = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3987"
      schemes?:        net.scheme_set
      allow_relative?: boolean ~ true
      allow_fragment?: boolean ~ true
      normalization?:  = NONE
    }

  }

  atom => !schema {
    @doc:"""
      A mixin for an atom constructor that follows a published specification:
      `spec` is its IRI, which the constructor pins.
      """
    specification => { spec: iri }

    @doc:"""
      ISO/IEC 11404: whether the value space has an order relation. Written on
      an atom, `@atom.ordering:TOTAL`.
      """
    ordering => @meta.annotation !enum [NONE PARTIAL TOTAL]

    @doc:"""
      ISO/IEC 11404: true when the value space has a least and a greatest value.
      """
    bounded => @meta.annotation boolean

    @doc:"""
      ISO/IEC 11404: true where a value is kept as written, false where it is
      rounded to a grid.
      """
    exact => @meta.annotation boolean

    @doc:"ISO/IEC 11404: marks a numeric type, written bare, `@atom.numeric`."
    numeric => @meta.annotation void

  }

  @doc:"""
    The type system: the constructors every type is built with, each named by
    its path, `meta.record`.
    """
  meta => !schema {
    @doc:"""
      The root of the type system: every constructor IS-A `top` ([TSON-SCHEMA]
      §4.1).
      """
    top       => abstract {}

    @doc:"""
      Where a type sits in the IS-A order. Derived, never written by a
      declaration: `parents` is an open record's parents as written,
      `supertypes` the closed IS-A chain, and `subtypes` its inverse
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §4.1, §8.1).
      """
    type_chain => {
      parents?:    [meta.type_expr]
      supertypes?: meta.set_type<meta.type_ref>
      subtypes?:   meta.set_type<meta.type_ref>
    }

    @doc:"""
      What every type's body is: `atom`, `product` and `sum` are its kinds, and
      `chain` its place in the IS-A order ([TSON-SCHEMA] §4.1).
      """
    type      => abstract meta.top & {
      chain?: meta.type_chain
    }

    @doc:"The kind of a scalar: a value written as one token."
    atom      => abstract meta.type & {}

    @doc:"The kind of a compound value: a record, an array, a map or a tuple."
    product   => abstract meta.type & {
      access_pattern: meta.product_access_type
      size_type:      meta.product_size_type
    }

    @doc:"""
      The kind of a value that is one of several types: a choice, a declared
      type or a dependent type.
      """
    sum       => abstract meta.type & {}

    @doc:"""
      Another name for an entry: `user_id => uuid` makes `user_id` the same type
      as `uuid` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §8.3).
      """
    alias => meta.top & {
      target: meta.type_ref
    }

    @doc:"""
      A parameter of an open record, `<T>`, or with a bound every argument must
      IS-A, `<T: text>` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10).
      """
    param => {
      name:   meta.param_name
      bound?: meta.type_ref
    }

    @doc:"The constructor of `void`: it admits only the void sentinel `_`."
    void_type => meta.atom & {}

    @doc:"""
      The default name: XID_Start, then XID_Continue or `-`, in NFC. The type of
      type, field and parameter names ([TSON-DATA] §7.7).
      """
    identifier => !unicode.identifier_type { continue_add: "-" }

    @doc:"A type's name: a schema-map key, and a segment of a reference."
    type_name  => meta.identifier

    @doc:"A field's name, in a record or a field group."
    field_name => meta.identifier

    @doc:"A parameter's name."
    param_name => meta.identifier

    @doc:"How a product's parts are reached: by position, or by name."
    product_access_type => !enum [INDEX NAMED]

    @doc:"Whether a product's number of parts is fixed by its type."
    product_size_type   => !enum [FIXED VARIABLE]

    @doc:"""
      What a field's `value` is for. FREE has none, DEFAULT is injected when the
      field is omitted, written `~ value`, and FIXED is the only value the field
      admits, written `= value` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2).
      """
    field_role          => !enum [FREE DEFAULT FIXED]

    @doc:"""
      How a record may be realised, written as a mark: `pet => abstract { … }`
      has no values of its own, `final { … }` admits no subtype, and OPEN is the
      unmarked default ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2).
      """
    record_extension_type => !enum [ABSTRACT FINAL OPEN]

    @doc:"""
      Marks a type as meant for use as an annotation:
      `title => @meta.annotation text` declares what `@title:"Order"` is read
      against. An alias of `void`, so it carries no force ([TSON-SCHEMA] §6).
      """
    annotation => void

    @doc:"""
      Resolver-attached to an entry's key, never written: the application of an
      open record the entry closes, `{ head: pair  arguments: [text integer] }`
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §8.2).
      """
    application => @meta.annotation meta.apply

    @doc:"""
      One field of a record, the spelling `name?: type? ~ value`. `optional` is
      the name's `?`, the key may be missing; `voidable` is the type's `?`, the
      value may be `_`; `role` and `value` are the default after `~` or the
      fixed value after `=`.

      `value` is read against the type `type` names, and is present exactly when
      `role` is not FREE. DEFAULT requires `optional`, and FIXED a field that is
      not `voidable` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2).
      """
    record_field => {
      type:      meta.type_expr
      optional?: boolean ~ false
      voidable?: boolean ~ false
      role?:     meta.field_role ~ FREE
      value?:    type!
    }

    @doc:"""
      A presence rule over a record's fields, written
      `( min: integer | exclusive_min: integer )?`. `members` lists the options,
      each the fields it holds: exactly one option is present, or at most one
      where `optional`. `optional_members` names the members that may be missing
      from a chosen option ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.11).
      """
    field_group => {
      members:           [[text; 1..]; 1..]
      optional_members?: [text; 1..]
      optional?:         boolean ~ false
    }

    @doc:"""
      The fields of a record: each field's name mapped to its facts, in
      declaration order.
      """
    field_map => !meta.map { key_type: text  value_type: meta.record_field  ordered: true }

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of every record type, written in braces:
      `person => { name: text  age?: integer }`.

      `fields` is flattened, a composition's fields being its operands' and its
      own. `parameters` makes it an open record, `<T> { item: T }`. `extension`
      is its `abstract` or `final` mark, `discriminators` names the fields its
      members are selected by, and `name_type` the type its field names are
      values of ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2; §5.8 for composition, §5.10 for open
      records).
      """
    record => meta.product & {
      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = NAMED
      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = FIXED
      parameters?:     [meta.param; 1..]
      fields:          meta.field_map
      groups?:         [meta.field_group]
      extension?:      meta.record_extension_type ~ OPEN
      discriminators?: [text; 1..]
      name_type?:      meta.identifier_ref
    }

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of `[T]`, with a size as `[T; 1..10]` and void elements as
      `[T?]`. `ordered` says whether two values differing only in order are
      distinct ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.3).
      """
    array => meta.product & {
      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = INDEX
      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = VARIABLE
      element_type:    meta.type_expr
      voidable?:       boolean ~ false
      ordered?:        boolean ~ true
      unique_items?:   boolean ~ false
      min_items?:      numeric.count
      max_items?:      numeric.count
    }

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of `set<T>`: an array whose elements are distinct and
      whose order is no part of its value ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.5).
      """
    set_type => meta.array ^ {
      voidable?:     = false
      ordered?:      = false
      unique_items?: = true
    }

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of `{K => V}`, with void values as `{K => V?}`; a key is
      never void. `ordered` is as `array`'s, and false unless the map says
      otherwise ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.3).
      """
    map => meta.product & {
      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = NAMED
      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = VARIABLE
      key_type:        meta.type_ref
      value_type:      meta.type_expr
      voidable?:       boolean ~ false
      ordered?:        boolean ~ false
      min_items?:      numeric.count
      max_items?:      numeric.count
    }

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of a namespace type, a map keyed by names:
      `api => !meta.namespace { key_type: meta.identifier  value_type: method }`,
      and then a value of it, `orders => !api { place => { … } }`. A segment of
      a qualified name selects a key of a namespace value, `orders.place`, and
      of no other map ([TSON-SCHEMA] §4.1, §5.12).
      """
    namespace => meta.map & {
      key_type:  meta.identifier_ref
      voidable?: = false
    }

    @doc:"One position of a tuple: its type, and whether its value may be void."
    tuple_element => {
      element_type: meta.type_expr
      voidable?:    boolean ~ false
    }

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of `[A, B]`: a fixed sequence of positions, each with a
      type of its own ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.3).
      """
    tuple => meta.product & {
      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = INDEX
      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = FIXED
      elements:        [meta.tuple_element]
    }

    @doc:"The members of an enum_type."
    enum_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: text  min_items: 1 }

    @doc:"""
      The constructor behind `enum`: a closed set of names, each a value of
      `name_type`. An author writes `!enum [OPEN DONE]`, and refines this
      constructor only for names of another naming convention ([TSON-SCHEMA]
      §7.4).
      """
    enum_type => meta.atom & {
      name_type: meta.identifier_ref
      members:   meta.enum_set
    }

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of `( A | B )`: a value of one of the variants. `disjoint`
      is derived, never written: whether the variants are told apart by form,
      without a tag ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.4).
      """
    choice => meta.sum & {
      variants:  [meta.type_expr]
      disjoint?: boolean
    }

    @doc:"""
      An author's assertion that a choice's variants are told apart by form,
      checked against the derived `disjoint` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.4).
      """
    disjoint => @meta.annotation void

    @doc:"""
      The open sum: a value names its own type by its tag, among the types of
      the closure ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.8).
      """
    declared_type => meta.sum & {}

    @doc:"""
      A value of any type the closure declares, named by its tag. What a schema
      map's entries are.
      """
    declared => !meta.declared_type {}

    @doc:"""
      A value is read against the type the sibling field `on` names. Written in
      place at a record field's `type`, `kind!` in source ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2).
      """
    dependent => final meta.sum & { on: text }

    @doc:"""
      An application, `box<T>` in source. Written in place it stands only inside
      an open record, with one of that record's parameters among its arguments;
      closed, it is the value of `@meta.application` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10).
      """
    apply => final meta.type & {
      head:      meta.type_ref
      arguments: [meta.type_ref; 1..]
    }

    @doc:"""
      A name held in a slot, written `handler: meta.ref<method>`. It names an
      entry whose value's type, past alias hops, is `of` or IS-A it: where `of`
      is a constructor the names are types', and otherwise they are values'
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §4.2, §5.12).
      """
    ref => meta.atom & {
      of: meta.type_ref
    }

    @doc:"A slot holding a type's name."
    type_ref => !meta.ref { of: meta.type }

    @doc:"""
      A slot holding the name of an identifier family, as a `name_type` does.
      """
    identifier_ref => !meta.type_ref ^ { of: unicode.identifier_type }

    @doc:"""
      A type written where it is used, `tags: [text]`, and not declared under a
      name ([TSON-SCHEMA] §4.2).
      """
    type_inline => { of: meta.type }

    @doc:"What a type slot holds: a type's name, or a type written in place."
    type_expr => @meta.disjoint ( meta.type_ref | meta.type_inline )

    @doc:"""
      A schema map: each name mapped to the type or value its entry resolves to
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.1, §8.1).
      """
    schema => !meta.namespace { key_type: meta.type_name  value_type: meta.declared }
  }

  binary => !schema {
    @doc:"RFC 4648's base encodings; HEX is its base16."
    bytes_encoding => !enum [BASE64 BASE64URL BASE32 HEX]

    @doc:"""
      The constructor of `bytes`. The value is the octets: equality and the
      length facets are over octets, never a spelling, so
      `sha256 => !bytes ^ { length: 32 }` is 32 octets. `encoding` picks the
      alphabet a text encoding spells them in and changes no value; another
      alphabet is another instance, `!binary.bytes_type { encoding: HEX }`
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5).
      """
    bytes_type => meta.atom & {
      encoding?:   binary.bytes_encoding ~ BASE64
      length?:     numeric.count
      min_length?: numeric.count
      max_length?: numeric.count
    }
  }

  @doc:"""
    The absence of a value, written `_` ([TSON-DATA] §2.9, [TSON-SCHEMA] §7.6).
    """
  void => !meta.void_type {}

  @doc:"`true` or `false`."
  boolean => !enum [true false]

  @doc:"""
    An integer of any size. Refine it for a range or a width:
    `age => !integer ^ { min: 0  max: 150 }` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5).
    """
  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:false @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric
  integer => !numeric.integer_type {}

  @doc:"Signed 64-bit integer."
  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true @atom.exact:true @atom.numeric
  int64 => !integer ^ { size: { bits: 64  signed: true } }

  @doc:"""
    Unicode text. Refine it to constrain it:
    `name => !text ^ { min_length: 1  max_length: 80 }` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5).
    """
  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false
  text => !unicode.text_type {}

  @doc:"""
    An octet sequence, written in base64 in TSON text. A digest is
    `sha256 => !bytes ^ { length: 32 }`, the length counting octets ([TSON-DATA]
    §5.3).
    """
  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false
  bytes => !binary.bytes_type {}

  @doc:"An IRI-reference (RFC 3987 §2.2): an IRI or a relative reference."
  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false
  iri_reference => !net.iri_type {}

  @doc:"An IRI (RFC 3987 §2.2): the scheme is required."
  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false
  iri => !iri_reference ^ { allow_relative: false }

  @doc:"A regular expression in I-Regexp (RFC 9485), the type of a `pattern`."
  @atom.ordering:NONE @atom.bounded:false
  regex => !unicode.regex_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Documentation for a declaration, a field or a document, written before it.
    The text is CommonMark 0.31.2, with no extensions, and raw HTML in it is
    never executed ([TSON-SCHEMA] §6).
    """
  doc => @meta.annotation text

  @doc:"""
    A closed set of names: `status => !enum [OPEN DONE]` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.4).
    """
  enum => meta.enum_type ^ { name_type?: = meta.identifier }

  @doc:"""
    `set<T>`: a collection of distinct elements whose order is no part of its
    value, `tags: set<text>` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.5).
    """
  set => meta.set_type

  @doc:"""
    The tag of a schema map: a schema document's root is `!schema { … }`, and so
    is a map nested as an entry, `geo => !schema { … }` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.1).
    """
  schema => meta.schema
}
